00:00:54.126 --> 00:00:58.011
Pond scum is kind of a rude name, isn’t
it?

00:00:58.011 --> 00:01:03.359
I mean, sure, it feels kind of appropriate
when you’re wading through murky waters,

00:01:03.359 --> 00:01:10.310
and you feel that disconcerting combination
of fuzzy and slick textures sliding against

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:12.548
the bottom of your feet.

00:01:12.548 --> 00:01:17.181
You might not be able to see it, 
but you can sure feel it—

00:01:17.181 --> 00:01:19.710
whether or not you want to.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:26.242
And it feels like anything could be there,
anything at all.

00:01:26.242 --> 00:01:27.643
But even still.

00:01:27.643 --> 00:01:28.736
Scum?

00:01:28.736 --> 00:01:30.951
That seems a little judgmental.

00:01:30.951 --> 00:01:37.290
Technically, the word “scum” was originally
used in the 14th century to describe the frothy

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:40.000
layers on top of liquids.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:46.170
It wasn’t until the 16th century that it began 
to be used more as an insult against people.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:52.399
So sure, “scum” was probably meant more
to describe the way pond scum obscures our

00:01:52.399 --> 00:01:56.938
water than as a moral criticism of those green
mats.

00:01:56.938 --> 00:01:59.634
But there is actually something kind of…

00:01:59.634 --> 00:02:02.494
scummy about pond scum.

00:02:03.721 --> 00:02:08.188
It’s not something they necessarily intend
to be.

00:02:08.188 --> 00:02:14.110
In fact, it’s a contradiction of sorts,
a process that takes them from an organism

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:21.000
that provides oxygen to its fellow pond neighbors,
to an organism that suffocates them.

00:02:22.053 --> 00:02:29.379
There are different types of green that accumulate
in our ponds, including clouds of cyanobacteria

00:02:29.379 --> 00:02:32.418
that disperse when you wave a stick through
them.

00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:35.108
But pond scum is different.

00:02:35.108 --> 00:02:41.000
If you drag a stick through pond scum, it’ll
come out of the water looking like a wet mat

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.400
woven together with green threads.

00:02:45.778 --> 00:02:50.855
It looks like that, because that is what pond
scum is.

00:02:50.855 --> 00:02:57.159
Those mats are made out of filamentous algae,
a range of species who bring a mixture of

00:02:57.159 --> 00:03:01.566
greens and yellows and browns to their joint
endeavor.

00:03:01.566 --> 00:03:08.126
Up close, with a microscope, you can see the
individual characteristics that make up those

00:03:08.126 --> 00:03:14.140
algae, almost as if you’re examining the
silk threads that have been stitched together

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:16.597
to form a glorious tapestry.

00:03:16.597 --> 00:03:21.772
And by “glorious tapestry,” we of course
mean “pond scum.”

00:03:22.975 --> 00:03:26.414
And yes, that feels like a contradiction.

00:03:26.414 --> 00:03:32.880
But look at these Spirogyra and their distinct
spiral shape that their chloroplasts wind

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:34.102
through them.

00:03:34.102 --> 00:03:37.865
They’re like a spectacular off-color candy
cane.

00:03:37.865 --> 00:03:42.850
When you feel pond scum on you, this is what
you’re feeling:

00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:49.850
beautiful microscopic strands built from chains
of cells that divide and divide to elongate

00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:53.068
the thread binding their lives together.

00:03:53.068 --> 00:03:59.170
Filamentous algae usually begin growing at
the bottom of ponds, in shallow areas where

00:03:59.170 --> 00:04:02.948
there is plenty of light to drive their photosynthesis.

00:04:02.948 --> 00:04:10.603
As the algae divides and extends its chains,
the filaments begin to tangle and weave together,

00:04:10.603 --> 00:04:13.112
forming a dense mat.

00:04:13.112 --> 00:04:19.530
Because the algae are photosynthesizing, they
are also releasing oxygen into the water—

00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:23.555
oxygen that gets trapped as bubbles within
the growing mat.

00:04:23.555 --> 00:04:29.009
Those bubbles lift the mat of filamentous
algae up so that it’s no longer trapped

00:04:29.009 --> 00:04:30.898
at the bottom of the pond.

00:04:30.898 --> 00:04:37.479
And that creates a sort of floating city,
with threads of algae that house many residents

00:04:37.479 --> 00:04:39.475
of the microcosmos.

00:04:39.475 --> 00:04:45.449
In the background here, you can see the blurry
form of a rotifer snapping up any snack that

00:04:45.449 --> 00:04:48.065
passes close enough for it to grasp.

00:04:48.065 --> 00:04:54.902
And here we can see a strange hypotrich popping
out from underneath a filament as it feeds,

00:04:54.902 --> 00:05:01.215
maybe using the algae like a log to hide under
before it ambushes its unsuspecting prey.

00:05:01.215 --> 00:05:08.090
And if you look hard enough, you might find
an aeolosoma, our favorite polka-dotted worm,

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:13.020
sandwiched between strands of algae, 
maybe casually wiggling around

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:16.169
on its way to a cannibalistic gathering.

00:05:16.169 --> 00:05:17.891
(Yes, that’s a thing.

00:05:17.891 --> 00:05:20.669
We have a whole episode about it, if you want
to know more.)

00:05:23.542 --> 00:05:29.820
All-in-all, it turns out that pond scum is
both made of beautiful algae threads, and

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:33.111
houses all sorts of fascinating creatures.

00:05:33.111 --> 00:05:37.551
You might be tempted to call it a little microbial
utopia.

00:05:37.551 --> 00:05:43.280
But James, our master of microscopes, often
gets emails from people who want to know how

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:48.127
to get rid of the scum growing in their artificial
ponds.

00:05:48.127 --> 00:05:52.460
“But they’re so wonderful,” you might
say, now that you know that underlying all

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:58.830
that muck is a wondrous world of worms and
rotifers and mysterious creatures housed in

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:01.397
a living home that sustains them.

00:06:01.397 --> 00:06:07.960
And yet every year, ponds around the world
become the site of a battle between the filamentous

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:11.675
algae that come to dominate them, and…

00:06:11.675 --> 00:06:15.000
well, pretty much everything else in the pond.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:22.030
This is a different situation from, say, the
cyanobacteria we mentioned earlier, or certain

00:06:22.030 --> 00:06:27.572
algal blooms, that can kill their fellow pond
residents by producing toxins.

00:06:27.572 --> 00:06:32.540
Filamentous algae wouldn’t do something
nefarious like poison their neighbors.

00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:34.551
They just suffocate them instead.

00:06:41.090 --> 00:06:46.628
The organisms living inside ponds rely on
oxygen that gets dissolved in the water.

00:06:46.628 --> 00:06:53.246
Some of that oxygen comes from outside the
pond, diffusing and circulating into the water.

00:06:53.246 --> 00:06:59.010
But of course, filamentous algae and their
fellow photosynthetic pond residents contribute

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:01.510
to the oxygen content as well.

00:07:01.510 --> 00:07:04.970
Which is quite kind of the filamentous algae.

00:07:04.970 --> 00:07:08.571
The problem is that they don’t just make
oxygen.

00:07:08.571 --> 00:07:10.983
They also consume it.

00:07:10.983 --> 00:07:16.199
And if the weather conditions aren’t quite
right, the algae will consume more oxygen

00:07:16.199 --> 00:07:17.668
than they make.

00:07:19.117 --> 00:07:24.580
This depletion can be exacerbated by nutrients
like phosphorus and nitrogen, which might

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:29.035
enter the pond from fertilizer or runoff from
around the pond.

00:07:29.035 --> 00:07:35.490
These nutrients cause the algae to grow and
grow and grow until their numbers skew the

00:07:35.490 --> 00:07:40.970
oxygen balance in their favor and against
everyone else’s.

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:44.120
And even if the algae die off all at once—

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:46.697
like through the use of a chemical algaecide—

00:07:46.697 --> 00:07:49.761
that doesn’t solve the oxygen problem.

00:07:49.761 --> 00:07:55.937
You’ve now provided the bacteria
in the pond plenty of dead algae to decompose,

00:07:55.937 --> 00:08:00.860
which means they consume a lot of oxygen instead.

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:04.520
That doesn’t mean that you can’t use chemicals
to kill algae.

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:10.449
However, it requires careful consideration
of the pond conditions and the algae involved

00:08:10.449 --> 00:08:14.794
to make sure that it makes sense to turn to
those algaecides.

00:08:14.794 --> 00:08:19.830
There are other methods for short-term control
of these algae populations.

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:25.750
Like physically removing them with a rake,
or using water-soluble dyes that limit sunshine

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:29.078
to the algae when they’re still at the bottom
of ponds.

00:08:30.643 --> 00:08:33.652
But these are short-term solutions.

00:08:33.652 --> 00:08:38.750
The fact is that long-term approaches means
preventing excess nutrients from entering

00:08:38.750 --> 00:08:44.649
the ponds in the first place, whether by thinking
through our fertilizer choices or adding buffers

00:08:44.649 --> 00:08:50.250
to our ponds that prevent runoff from entering
or any number of other approaches that help

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:52.621
maintain a healthy pond.

00:08:52.621 --> 00:08:59.050
Because maybe in the long-term, dealing with
filamentous algae is rooted in acknowledging

00:08:59.050 --> 00:09:04.460
the way they suit the word “scum” in all
senses of the word.

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:11.410
They are grimy and gross, but also something
that is simply a part of the water.

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:18.320
And in between the negative connotations of
the word and its more neutral origins is something

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:23.599
wondrous that requires balance on our end
to maintain.

00:09:30.253 --> 00:09:36.721
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as 
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

